*              remote-admin.ps1 -On answers 4: only SDSYS, or a caller holding its password, can register the broker task that runs as
*              SDSYS (measured).  The password goes to the helper inside the script !ps_script_out writes to its restricted file, as
*              !set_passwd's does, never on a command line, and is cleared at once.
* 13 Nov 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 202, Linux parity (owner, 13 Nov: Linux's remote sdsys runs them): RUN runs REMOTE.SSH {ON | OFF}
*              and REMOTE.API {ON | LOCAL | OFF}, each also with no keyword, seven verbs; REMOTE.API's restart question is answered N
*              (the console's Enter: nothing is restarted, the listener moves at SD's next start).
* 13 Nov 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 202: carried from Linux (S.95 stage 1b, S.101, S.106) in the Windows form of
*              .claude/design-remote-admin-20261113.md and the owner's answers of 13 Nov 2026 (Linux parity unless the OS forbids).  ON | OFF
*              open and shut ssh for a SEPARATE account, SDRADMIN, not for SDSYS: SDSYS is an elevated Windows administrator, and its password
*              over the network would be the machine's (OS reason).  ON makes SDRADMIN the first time (CREATE.ACCOUNT's own body, the Windows
*              password asked twice here) and then runs remote-admin.ps1 -On; OFF runs -Off and logs every SDRADMIN session out.  The helper is
*              remote-admin.ps1 through SDSYS's elevated helper, in place of "sudo -n sd-elevate sdsys-ssh".  Windows additions: AUDIT {n} and
*              ACCOUNTS (the remote session has no shell to read them with), and RUN, the broker's job (sd-radmin.ps1): one listed account
*              command with its password, if any, read from standard input and given to the verb through the DATA queue.
*              Messages are Linux's + 1005 (11923 -> 12928, 11924 -> 12929), naming SDRADMIN, except 11929, whose slot 12934 the AI took the
